What does this code do?
while (*p)
  p++ |= ' ';

As written, this is invalid C/C++:

```c
while (*p)
    p++ |= ' ';
```

It is parsed as `p++ |= ' ';`, but `p++` cannot be assigned to, so it will not compile.

If the intended code was:

```c
while (*p)
    *p++ |= ' ';
```

then it walks through a null-terminated string, bitwise-ORs each character with a space (`' '`), and advances `p` each time.

For an ASCII buffer, check the uppercase range explicitly:

```c
while (*p != '\0') {
    unsigned char c = (unsigned char)*p;

    if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z')
        *p = (char)(c + ('a' - 'A'));

    ++p;
}
```

This changes only `A` through `Z`; all other bytes are left unchanged.

For `tolower`, include `<ctype.h>` and cast the input to `unsigned char`:

```c
while (*p != '\0') {
    *p = (char)tolower((unsigned char)*p);
    ++p;
}
```

The cast is required because the `<ctype.h>` functions are defined for `EOF` or values representable as `unsigned char`; passing a negative `char` value directly can cause undefined behavior. `tolower` is locale-aware and may lowercase characters beyond ASCII `A`–`Z`, so use the explicit range check when only those letters should change.

