## Aim

To solve a system of simultaneous linear equations using the Gauss Elimination Method.

## Objectives

1. To convert the coefficient matrix into an upper-triangular matrix.
2. To obtain the unknown variables using back substitution.
3. To implement Gauss elimination with partial pivoting in MATLAB.
4. To compare the calculated solution with the exact solution using a graph.

## Algorithm

Given a system of equations:

\[
[A][x]=[b]
\]

1. Form the augmented matrix \([A\; b]\).
2. Select the largest available pivot in the current column.
3. Interchange rows if necessary.
4. Eliminate the elements below the pivot using:
   \[
   R_i \leftarrow R_i-\frac{a_{ik}}{a_{kk}}R_k
   \]
5. Repeat the elimination process for all columns.
6. The augmented matrix becomes an upper-triangular matrix.
7. Apply back substitution:
   \[
   x_i=\frac{b_i-\sum_{j=i+1}^{n}a_{ij}x_j}{a_{ii}}
   \]
8. Display the solution and plot the calculated values against the exact values.

## MATLAB Code

```matlab
clc;
clear;
close all;

% Coefficient matrix and right-hand-side vector
A = [10  -1   2;
     -1  11  -1;
      2  -1  10];

b = [14; 18; 30];

% Exact solution for comparison
xExact = [1; 2; 3];

% Form the augmented matrix
Aug = [A b];
n = length(b);

% Forward elimination with partial pivoting
for k = 1:n-1

    % Find the largest pivot in the current column
    [~, p] = max(abs(Aug(k:n, k)));
    p = p + k - 1;

    % Check for a zero pivot
    if abs(Aug(p, k)) < 1e-12
        error('The system has no unique solution.');
    end

    % Interchange rows when required
    if p ~= k
        temp = Aug(k, :);
        Aug(k, :) = Aug(p, :);
        Aug(p, :) = temp;
    end

    % Eliminate elements below the pivot
    for i = k+1:n
        factor = Aug(i, k) / Aug(k, k);
        Aug(i, k:end) = Aug(i, k:end) - ...
                        factor * Aug(k, k:end);
    end
end

% Check the last pivot
if abs(Aug(n, n)) < 1e-12
    error('The system has no unique solution.');
end

% Back substitution
x = zeros(n, 1);

for i = n:-1:1
    rhs = Aug(i, end);

    if i < n
        rhs = rhs - Aug(i, i+1:n) * x(i+1:n);
    end

    x(i) = rhs / Aug(i, i);
end

% Display results
disp('Upper-triangular augmented matrix:');
disp(Aug);

disp('Calculated solution:');
disp(x);

% Calculate residual
residual = norm(A*x - b);
fprintf('Residual norm = %.6e\n', residual);

% Plot calculated and exact solutions
figure;
bar([x, xExact]);
grid on;

xlabel('Variable number');
ylabel('Solution value');
title('Gauss Elimination: Calculated vs Exact Solution');
legend('Calculated Solution', 'Exact Solution', 'Location', 'northwest');

set(gca, 'XTickLabel', {'x_1', 'x_2', 'x_3'});
```

## Interpretation

The coefficient matrix is transformed into an upper-triangular matrix by eliminating the elements below each pivot. Back substitution then gives the solution:

\[
x_1=1,\qquad x_2=2,\qquad x_3=3
\]

The calculated bars in the graph should coincide with the exact-solution bars, indicating that the method has produced accurate results. The residual norm should be very small, showing that the calculated solution satisfies the original equations. Partial pivoting improves the reliability of the method by selecting a suitable pivot at each elimination step.

